{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Build an isolated exact sparse-obstacle candidate. Deduplicate blocked coordinates; return 0 if (0,0) or (n-1,n-1) is blocked. Precompute factorials/inverse factorials through 2n-2 and compute paths with C(dx+dy,dx). Sort blocked cells by (y,x), append the target, and use inclusion-exclusion: ways(p)=C(x+y,x)-sum over earlier blocked q dominated by p of ways(q)*C((x-xq)+(y-yq),x-xq), modulo MOD. This removes the source's O(n^2) grid allocation and work when M is small, including the O(n) no-obstacle case. Use int64 modular products, fast input, and differential tests including duplicate obstacles, endpoint obstacles, same-row/column obstacles, and arbitrary valid layouts.\",\"B\":\"Research a separate parallel version of the same exact sparse recurrence rather than a speculative approximation. Group blocked points by x+y: a dominated predecessor always has a strictly smaller diagonal, so all outputs within one diagonal can safely compute concurrently from completed earlier diagonals. Give each worker a private 64-bit accumulation and reduce modulo MOD; avoid parallel overhead for small groups and retain a serial fallback. Measure whether four ARM64 cores overcome the recurrence's memory traffic and synchronization. Preserve deduplication and all endpoint handling; do not assume obstacle density, input ordering, or benchmark values.\",\"C\":\"Produce and assess an exact dense/hybrid fallback for regimes where O(M^2) inclusion-exclusion loses. Replace vector<vector<bool>>(n,n) with sorted obstacle columns per row (O(M+n) metadata) and retain only rolling O(n) DP rows; process open row segments without an n-by-n blocked allocation. Consider a carefully validated segmented-prefix implementation for row parallelism, but keep a low-overhead serial rolling implementation as the dense reference. Establish a measured, input-shape-based crossover between dense O(n^2) work and sparse O(M^2) work; no fixed-test specialization. Also compare every candidate against the supplied semantics for coordinates and modulo arithmetic.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only a candidate that is both correct and faster under the existing build/run conditions; the observed correct baseline is already 0.080853 s, so combined changes may regress. Prefer the sparse combinatorial path for its asymptotic elimination of the current n^2 storage/work, with a measured dense fallback only if C demonstrates a real crossover. Preserve stdin/stdout exactly, print one modular integer and newline, use the existing compiler/script/budgets unchanged, and validate randomized small cases against the original DP plus boundary cases. Do not blindly combine parallel and hybrid changes: retain the fastest validated complete implementation for every valid input.\",\"analysis\":\"The active source is Problem D, not A-C: it counts monotone paths on an n-by-n grid with blocked cells. Its rolling DP still allocates blockedCells at O(n^2) and executes O(n^2) recurrence steps, which is incompatible with the stated n bound even when obstacles are sparse. The key exact optimization is path-count inclusion-exclusion over blocked cells using binomial coefficients modulo 1,000,000,007; it has O(n+M^2) time and O(n+M) memory, while a dense rolling-row fallback costs O(n^2) time and O(n+M) memory. The only automatic timing is a correct 0.080853 s baseline over three samples, so all proposals must be measured independently and none should be presumed additive.\"}"}}
